/// Semantic authorization — the interface a component calls before doing
/// something the host cannot see it doing.
///
/// ACT's capability system enforces physical classes by interception: the host
/// sits on the `wasi:filesystem`, `wasi:http` and `wasi:sockets` boundaries and
/// decides every crossing. Semantic actions have no such boundary. A
/// `DROP DATABASE` travels to Postgres over an already-granted socket; a script
/// injection travels to a browser over an already-granted HTTP session. The
/// host sees bytes on a permitted channel and nothing more.
///
/// So the initiative comes from the component. It names the class of action it
/// is about to take, and the host applies that class's policy — the same
/// policy surface, the same grants, the same audit trail as every physical
/// class. What the host cannot verify is that the component asked at all: this
/// interface defends against a mistaken or manipulated *agent* driving a
/// trusted component, not against a component that lies about what it is doing.
/// That second threat is what the physical capability ceiling is for, and the
/// two mechanisms are meant to be used together.
///
/// The class MUST appear in the component's `act:component` capability
/// declarations. An undeclared class is refused without consulting the operator
/// and no grant can widen it, so the manifest of a signed artifact is a
/// complete, inspectable list of the semantic actions it can ever ask for.
///
/// Like `act:credentials`, this package runs against the ecosystem's usual
/// direction: the host implements it and the component imports it. It is
/// opt-in in both directions — a component that never calls `request` need not
/// import it, and a host that implements it refuses classes the component never
/// declared.
///
/// The data model lives in its own `types` interface, function-free and
/// async-free, so SDK helpers and sync-shim adapters can `use act:consent/types`
/// without pulling in the async signature.
package act:consent@0.1.0;

/// Consent data model — function-free, async-free, safe to `use` anywhere.
interface types {
  use act:core/types@0.4.0.{cbor};

  /// The host's answer.
  ///
  /// An enum rather than a `result` because refusal is an ordinary outcome, not
  /// a failure: the component is expected to branch on `deny`, skip the action
  /// and return a useful tool error. Every reason for refusal collapses to
  /// `deny` on purpose — an undeclared class, a denied grant, a human answering
  /// no, and a host with no way to ask all produce the same value, so a
  /// component cannot probe the operator's policy by varying its requests.
  enum decision {
    allow,
    deny,
  }

  /// One request for authorization.
  record consent-request {
    /// The capability class this action belongs to, as a namespaced
    /// identifier — "db:drop", "browser:navigate". It MUST be declared in the
    /// component's `act:component` capabilities; an undeclared class is denied
    /// without a prompt.
    ///
    /// Classes are the unit an operator grants, denies and reads in an audit
    /// trail, so they should name what a person would want to decide about.
    /// Splitting an irreversible action into its own class ("db:drop" apart
    /// from "db:ddl") is what lets that action be refused without refusing
    /// the rest.
    class: string,
    /// The subject within the class — a database name, an origin, an account
    /// identifier.
    ///
    /// It does three jobs at once, deliberately. It is what the host remembers
    /// when a human approves ("allow this, don't ask again"), it is what the
    /// audit trail records, and it is a dimension policy constraints match
    /// against. Because the same value serves all three, the operation a human
    /// approved cannot differ from the operation policy authorized, and a
    /// second `key` hidden inside `args` cannot shadow it.
    ///
    /// An empty key means the decision is not subject-specific, so approving it
    /// approves the class. Prefer a real subject: a remembered "yes" to a whole
    /// class is exactly the blanket authorization this interface exists to
    /// avoid.
    ///
    /// The component chooses this string, which makes it untrusted input. A
    /// class SHOULD fix the shape of its key — an origin rather than a full
    /// URL, a bare identifier rather than a path — so that operator patterns
    /// written against it mean what they appear to mean.
    key: string,
    /// One line, in the component's own words, describing the action to the
    /// human who may be asked to approve it.
    ///
    /// Untrusted, and treated as such: the host attributes it to the component,
    /// strips control characters, truncates it, and never lets it stand alone
    /// as the question. It supplies detail the host cannot know — which rows,
    /// which account, why now — and it never determines the decision.
    summary: string,
    /// Further dimensions policy may constrain, as a CBOR map — for example
    /// `{ "table": "events" }`. Usually empty: `key` alone carries most
    /// classes.
    ///
    /// A value that is not a CBOR map carries no dimensions rather than being
    /// an error; `key` still matches. Nothing here can widen what `key` and the
    /// declared ceiling already permit.
    args: cbor,
  }
}

/// Host-provided semantic authorization.
interface consent-authority {
  use types.{consent-request, decision};
  use act:core/types@0.4.0.{metadata};

  /// Ask the host to authorize a semantic action.
  ///
  /// Returns a decision, never a trap. On `deny` the component skips the action
  /// and degrades gracefully — typically returning a tool error carrying
  /// `std:capability-denied`. A component MUST NOT perform the action it asked
  /// about after receiving `deny`.
  ///
  /// `meta` carries the calling context, including `std:session-id` where the
  /// component is session-scoped. It anchors the audit record; it does not
  /// select policy, which is keyed on the class and the key alone.
  ///
  /// `async` by design. Today a host may answer from static policy and return
  /// immediately, but the same call may need to reach a human — a terminal
  /// prompt, an MCP elicitation, a GUI dialog — in the middle of an in-flight
  /// tool call. The async signature is what lets that happen without a
  /// breaking change to this interface.
  ///
  /// The host MUST record every request and its outcome in the audit trail.
  request: async func(req: consent-request, meta: metadata) -> decision;
}
